  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 345 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nDry and unseasonably mild to warm conditions will be the rule for \ncentral Indiana this week as persistent ridging directs better \nforcing to the west and north.  High pressure will remain stacked \nfrom the Midwest to the Mid-Atlantic through the early week... \ncontinuing calm to very light winds that may occasionally drift out \nof the south, maintaining low early-autumn type humidity with \ndewpoints nearing 50F.  Trend for greater cloud cover, albeit higher \ndecks as the southern edge of moisture conveyor from the Plains to \nGreat Lakes catches the area.  Diurnal ranges to extend from 40s pre-\ndawn to afternoons 70-75F.\n\nOvercast conditions possible around the late Tuesday/early Wednesday \ntimeframe as small short wave lifts by the region..above a dry \ncolumn unable to produce any precipitation.  Next air mass will then \ntransverse US-Canada border, holding chilly conditions to in New \nEngland before its center also slides down to the Mid-Atlantic by \nthe end of the workweek.  Noticeably stronger, yet reasonable \nsurface gradient will return more seasonable breeze for the second \nhalf of the forecast period...with southerly gusts up to 10-15 mph \non Thursday and Friday combining with ample sun to boost readings \nabove 75 degrees, nearing daily records in the low 80s.\n\nRain showers are expected to finally reach central Indiana by next \nweekend when progression of a couple short waves strengthen and pick \nup adequate Gulf moisture while approaching IN from the southwest. A \nconfluent downstream upper flow should keep the waves from departing \nIndiana too quickly...although any moderate or greater rainfall may \nbe delayed to later next week when better forcing is favored while \nelevated precipitable water is still present.",
